{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>These are the accounts of, etc.</b> The saying of <b>אֵלֶּה</b> comes to disqualify all counts in the world, for whatever a person counts of imaginary possessions — his count is no count, and its name testifies against him: what are you counting? But this count stands forever. And the reason is that it is the count of the Mishkan, the wondrous one, in which dwelt the Elohim of the world, HaShem." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzia 42a): blessing rests neither in something measured nor in something counted — and here it says <b>אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי</b>, meaning, to these there is a counting yet no rigor (i.e., no concern that counting forestalls blessing), for this count — on the contrary — its counting brings blessing, for every detail that increases in number magnifies the merit and draws blessing around the one who increases. And the reason is that it belongs to the Mishkan." "<b>Further,</b> by saying <b>אֵלֶּה</b> it alludes to what our Sages of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 21:5): if you made bundles [of transgression], etc. — make corresponding bundles of mitzvot, for a person must repair the sin in kind. Now Israel sinned and said (Exodus 32:4): ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״; therefore this <b>אֵלֶּה</b> came in the aspect of merit to atone for that sin. And you will find that they said (Shemot Rabbah) that the testimony — that is, the Mishkan — served as testimony for them that the sin of the Calf was forgiven. And when you examine closely, you will find that every detail of the sin of the Calf has its counterpart in the Mishkan: they craved a craving (Exodus 32:1) for elohim that would go before them — behold, they prepared a place for HaShem, the true Elohim, to be before them. They built an altar and brought burnt offerings (ibid. v. 6) — behold, they prepared an altar to bring burnt offerings and peace offerings to HaShem. They gave their gold — behold, they gave their gold and their silver and all their precious vessels to HaShem. They took off their earrings — behold, they brought the men upon the women: not earrings alone, but all their jewelry from upon them and every precious vessel, as we explained in parashat Vayakhel (Exodus 35:22). They appointed officers over it to make it — behold, they appointed Betzalel to make, etc. They said ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — behold, <b>אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי</b> for atonement." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along this line: because Moshe made the accounting of what Israel brought into the House of HaShem, therefore the Torah came and vindicated him and said <b>אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי</b> etc., meaning, along the lines of what it says (Leviticus 23:2): ״אֵלֶּה הֵם מוֹעֲדָי״ — whatever count HaShem vindicates: ״אֲשֶׁר פֻקַּד עַל פִּי מֹשֶׁה״. It was precise in saying <b>עַל פִּי מֹשֶׁה</b> and did not say ״אֲשֶׁר פָּקַד מֹשֶׁה״ — the reason being that there was no flaw at all with him requiring vindication; rather, from his mouth we live, as it is written (Exodus 36:3): ״וַיִּקְחוּ מִלִּפְנֵי מֹשֶׁה״ — that he alone received it all." "And the reason of Moshe for bringing himself under suspicion — perhaps this suspicion never entered his mind to guard against it, for how could they suspect him when HaShem spoke with him at all times? If, heaven forbid, any wrong were found in him, the Elohim who knows hidden things would reject him — and needless to say that Elohim would no longer speak with him. Or, examine closely what HaShem said to him (Exodus 25:2): ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְיִקְחוּ לִי״ etc., as we explained in its place — for He commanded him that he alone should be the treasurer. And the ways of HaShem are straight, and the righteous walk in them even though the rebellious stumble in them." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>these are the reckonings of the Mishkan</b> — and who justifies that this is so and not more? Therefore it says ״מִשְׁכַּן הָעֵדוּת״ — meaning, the Mishkan itself testifies to the matter, after the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 52) on the verse ״וַיָּקֶם מֹשֶׁה אֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן״ — that not one of Israel was able to set it up. And this is their language: How many sages were there, and they could not erect the Mishkan, etc. And they did not know how to set it in place; each one took his handiwork and went to Moses, etc., and the holy spirit rested upon Moses and he erected the Mishkan — end quote. This is its testimony: that he was clean of hands, that there is no violence in his palms, for the Holy One blessed be He does not perform a miracle for doers of wrong, and especially in the very place where the wrong was done, heaven forbid." "And if you say: What testimony is this? One could object that since Moses was a mighty man of valor, his strength stood him in stead to set it up — what others did not have the power to do. Therefore he concluded <b>the service of the Levites by the hand of Itamar</b>, etc. — meaning, do you not find that the service of the Levites which is by the hand of Itamar is the planks of the Mishkan and its bars and its pillars and its sockets, and they are the ones who take it down when they journey and set it up when they camp, as it is written in the portion BaMidbar (Numbers 1:51): ״וּבַחֲנֹת הַמִּשְׁכָּן יָקִימוּ אֹתוֹ הַלְוִיִּם״ — see that the Levites would dismantle it and set it up; if so, it was not lack of strength that was the cause. And according to this we have clarified the intent of the verse's saying <b>the service of the Levites</b> etc. <b>by the hand of Itamar</b>, which was not known what it comes to teach and what relevance it has to this statement here, and why it said only Itamar and not Elazar the great — and according to our approach it comes out correctly." "But still one could object from the side of wisdom, that Moses was wise and sharp and knew how to set it up, and no miracle was performed at all. Therefore it says <b>and Betzalel son of Uri</b> etc. <b>made all that HaShem commanded</b>, and they of blessed memory said that even things Moses did not tell him, his mind concurred with, <b>and with him Oholiav</b> etc. <b>craftsman</b> etc. — and even so his wisdom did not avail him, as they said in the Midrash (Exodus Rabbah 52) that even the craftsmanship of the craftsmen did not help. And according to this we have clarified the intent of the verse in informing us of the deed of Betzalel after we already knew this two and three times in the portion Vayakhel — rather it comes to strengthen the testimony of the miracle of the Mishkan, to say: from this you learn that this was nothing but a miracle, and this is the testimony of the Mishkan concerning its reckonings. And along this line, the verse's saying <b>as was reckoned by the mouth of Moses</b> is to say that on this account the testimony came to remove suspicion from him." "<b>Further</b>, it intends after the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 51) that the Mishkan atoned for the deed of the Calf. They further said in Ḥullin (Ḥullin 5a) that idolatry is weighed against the entire Torah, and this is the verse's saying ״אֵלֶּה פְקוּדֵי״ — meaning, in the manner that Onkelos translates (Leviticus 27:34) ״אֵלֶּה הַמִּצְוֹת״ — ״אִלֵּין פִּקּוֹדַיָא״ — the commandments that depend upon the Mishkan. <b>The Mishkan of the Testimony, as was reckoned</b> etc. — meaning, all the commandments in the Torah that Moses was commanded — all of them are present in the commandments of the Mishkan, and thereby the Mishkan atones for the Calf, which is weighed against the entire Torah. And perhaps it intends to allude to the matter through the reckoning of the reckonings of the Mishkan, which totals six hundred and twelve (תרי״ה), and they are the 613 commandments and the two tablets — and from this you will find that they were arranged in the reckonings of the Mishkan." "And the verse's saying <b>the service of the Levites</b> comes to guard the matter — lest you say: since the sin of the Calf has been atoned, which was what brought about the removal of service from the firstborn, as they of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 12), if so the crown returns to its former state and the firstborn will serve the service of the Mishkan. Therefore it says immediately after this <b>the service of the Levites</b> — meaning, this service belongs only to the Levites. Here it included the entire tribe of Levi, both the priests and the Levites, for the priests too are called Levites, as it is written (Deuteronomy 27:9) ״וְהַכֹּהֲנִים הַלְוִיִּם״." "And the verse's saying <b>under the hand of Itamar</b> etc. — meaning: it goes without saying the elevated services of the priests, and it goes without saying the elevated services of the Levites, those of the sons of Kehat and the sons of Gershon; but even the lightest service of the Levites, <b>under the hand of Itamar</b> — namely, the boards and the sockets — holds in its proper place. And from this you learn that the sin of the calf was not erased with an erasure whose trace is unrecognized, as the trustworthy witness testifies (Exodus 32:34): ״וּבְיוֹם פָּקְדִי״ etc." "<b>Further</b>, it alludes by saying <b>the appointed duties of the Tabernacle</b> in the sense of appointment, on the pattern of the verse (Numbers 4:16): ״וּפְקֻדַּת אֶלְעָזָר״, which is the language of appointment over the matter. And the verse informs us of all the appointments of the Tabernacle: some are appointed over the Tabernacle itself, for its continued existence or its annulment, Heaven forbid; some over its service; some over those who serve in it; and some over its craft-work.\n\nAnd it begins with the appointment for the continued existence of the Tabernacle, saying <b>the Tabernacle of the Testimony</b> — meaning: the appointment of the Tabernacle is the testimony, which is the Torah, for as long as Israel engages in the Torah and fulfills its commandments the Tabernacle endures, and without this there is no Tabernacle. And this is what they said, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 12), on the verse (Psalms 78:60): ״אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן בָּאָדָם״ — did He really pitch His tent in a man? Rather, through the instrumentality of man, if they prepare their heart to study Torah and fulfill its commandments. And not specifically the written testimony inscribed on the tablets and in the written scroll, but rather <b>which was appointed by the mouth of Moses</b> — meaning, the Oral Torah that was given to Moses orally and not for writing.\n\nSecond, it alludes to the appointment of Moses, for he was appointed in this matter to command the laws and rules of the Tabernacle.\n\nThird appointment: those who serve in the Tabernacle — ״the service of the Levites״ — meaning, they are appointed over the service of the Tabernacle.\n\nFourth appointment: they are Aaron and his sons, and all three of them the verse alludes to with <b>Itamar and Aaron</b> — these are stated explicitly, and Elazar is alluded to by saying <b>son of Aaron</b>, for I already know that Itamar is the son of Aaron; rather, it is to allude to Elazar. Or perhaps the Torah did not mention Aaron at all in the appointment of the Tabernacle, only his sons, and saying <b>son of Aaron the priest</b> alludes to Elazar — and it did not mention Aaron because he still lacked rectification for the deed of the calf that he made; for afterward his two sons died and he brought his atonement on the eighth day of the inauguration, and therefore it did not mention his appointment but rather mentioned his name together with his son.\n\nFifth appointment: the one who performs the craft-work of the Tabernacle, and that is what it says: <b>and Betzalel</b> etc. <b>made</b> etc. <b>and with him Oholiav</b> etc.\n\nYou thus find that those appointed in this particular aspect of the Tabernacle are five: (1) the Torah, which governs its continued existence, whether it will endure or not; (2) Moses, appointed over its commandments — in what manner it shall be made and conducted; (3) the Levites, to serve, to dismantle, to load, etc.; (4) the priests, to perform the services and to be appointed over the Levites in their service; (5) Betzalel and Oholiav, over the craft-work. And concerning all of these it said: <b>These are the appointed duties of the Tabernacle.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {8 "The gematria calculation: the verse counts items totaling a number whose letters Or HaChaim reads as תרי״ה (612), which together with the two tablets equals תרי״ג (613) commandments."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:03.053237481Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}